                "content": "{\"final_answer\": 4049, \"method\": \"none\", \"solution\": \"### Summary ###\\n\\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is 4048.\\n\\n**b. Method Sketch:**\\n- Model the uncovered squares by a permutation $p$ of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$, where $n=2025$.\\n- Show that any rectangle covering squares in row $i$ must lie entirely to the left of the uncovered square $(i,p(i))$ or entirely to the right of it; it cannot cross column $p(i)$.\\n- Consequently each row $i$ needs at least one tile for the left segment (if $p(i)\\\\neq1$) and at least one tile for the right segment (if $p(i)\\\\neq n$).\\n- Since exactly one row has $p(i)=1$ and exactly one row has $p(i)=n$, the total number of required tiles is at least $2n-2$.\\n- Construct a tiling that attains this bound by, for every row $i$, placing a horizontal tile covering the left segment (when non‑empty) and another covering the right segment (when non‑empty). These tiles are disjoint and cover all squares except the uncovered ones.\\n- The construction uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles, matching the lower bound.\\n- Hence the minimum number of tiles is $2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048$.\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\nLet $n=2025$.  Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares can be identified with a permutation $p$ of $\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$: in row $i$ the uncovered square lies in column $p(i)$.  Write the uncovered square as the point $(i,p(i))$.\\n\\n#### 1.  A lower bound\\n\\nConsider a fixed row $i$.  The uncovered square $(i,p(i))$ splits the row into two (possibly empty) contiguous blocks:\\n\\\\[\\nL_i = \\\\{(i,j) : 1\\\\le j < p(i)\\\\},\\\\qquad R_i = \\\\{(i,j) : p(i) < j \\\\le n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nAny rectangular tile that contains a square of row $i$ must be a sub‑rectangle of the grid.  If such a tile intersected both $L_i$ and $R_i$, then its column interval would contain $p(i)$, and therefore the tile would also contain the uncovered square $(i,p(i))$, which is forbidden.  Hence every tile that meets row $i$ is contained entirely in $L_i$ or entirely in $R_i$.\\n\\nIf $L_i$ is non‑empty (i.e. $p(i)\\\\neq1$) at least one tile is needed to cover the squares of $L_i$, because a tile cannot cross the column $p(i)$.  Similarly, if $R_i$ is non‑empty (i.e. $p(i)\\\\neq n$) at least one tile is needed for $R_i$.\\n\\nThus the total number $T$ of tiles satisfies\\n\\\\[\\nT \\\\ge \\\\bigl|\\\\{i : p(i)\\\\neq1\\\\}\\\\bigr| + \\\\bigl|\\\\{i : p(i)\\\\neq n\\\\}\\\\bigr|.\\n\\\\]\\nSince $p$ is a permutation, exactly one index $i$ satisfies $p(i)=1$ and exactly one index $j$ satisfies $p(j)=n$.  Consequently\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl|\\\\{i : p(i)\\\\neq1\\\\}\\\\bigr| = n-1,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\bigl|\\\\{i : p(i)\\\\neq n\\\\}\\\\bigr| = n-1,\\n\\\\]\\nand we obtain the universal lower bound\\n\\\\[\\nT \\\\ge (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\nFor $n=2025$ this gives $T\\\\ge4048$.\\n\\n#### 2.  An attainable construction\\n\\nWe now exhibit a tiling that uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles, thereby showing the lower bound is tight.\\n\\nFor each row $i$ we place:\\n- a horizontal tile covering the whole block $L_i$ if $L_i$ is non‑empty;\\n- a horizontal tile covering the whole block $R_i$ if $R_i$ is non‑empty.\\n\\nEach of $L_i$ and $R_i$ is a contiguous interval of columns, so the placed objects are indeed rectangles.  Different rows are disjoint, so the tiles do not overlap.  By construction every unit square that is not one of the uncovered squares $(i,p(i))$ belongs to exactly one of the tiles.\\n\\nThe number of tiles used equals the number of non‑empty left blocks plus the number of non‑empty right blocks, which is precisely $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$, because the only rows with an empty left block are those with $p(i)=1$ (there is exactly one such row) and the only rows with an empty right block are those with $p(i)=n$ (again exactly one row).\\n\\nThus we have a tiling with $2n-2$ tiles, matching the lower bound.\\n\\n#### 3.  Conclusion\\n\\nBoth the lower bound and the explicit construction give the same value.  Hence the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n2n-2 = 2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore Matilda needs at least $4048$ tiles, and $4048$ tiles are sufficient.\"}"
