uares except the $n$ uncovered ones.  For each rectangle $\\\\mathcal R$ let $J(\\\\mathcal R)$ be its set of columns.  If $J(\\\\mathcal R)$ contains two distinct hole‑columns $c_1<c_2$, then $\\\\mathcal R$ cannot contain any row whose hole lies in $\\\\{c_1,c_2\\\\}$; consequently the rows of $\\\\mathcal R$ are a proper subset of the whole set of rows.  In particular a rectangle that uses a column which is a hole for some row can be used only for the rows whose holes lie **strictly** to the opposite side of that column.\\n\\nNow look at the set $E$ of even‑numbered columns $\\\\{2,4,\\\\dots,2024\\\\}$.  For a rectangle whose column interval contains an even column $e\\\\in E$, the rows of that rectangle must all have their holes either left of $e$ or right of $e$, otherwise the rectangle would contain the hole of a row whose hole is $e$.\\n\\nIf a rectangle uses an even column $e$ as a *middle* column (i.e., the rectangle’s column interval is exactly $\\\\{e\\\\}$), then the rows covered by the rectangle are precisely those rows whose holes are on opposite sides of $e$.  Such a rectangle can replace the two separate left‑ and right‑rectangles that would be needed for those two rows.  Hence each even column can contribute at most **one** saving compared with the naive bound $2n-2$.\\n\\nThere are $\\\\lfloor n/2\\\\rfloor=1012$ even columns, so at most $1012$ savings are possible.  Starting from the trivial lower bound $2n-2$ (obtained by covering each row’s left part and right part separately) we obtain\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{minimum number of rectangles}\\\\ \\\\ge\\\\ 2n-2-\\\\bigl\\\\lfloor\\\\frac n2\\\\bigr\\\\rfloor\\n\\\\ =\\\\ 2\\\\cdot2025-2-1012\\\\ =\\\\ 3036.\\n\\\\]\\nThus any tiling needs at least $3036$ tiles.\\n\\n#### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\\n\\nWe now exhibit a placement of the uncovered squares and a tiling with exactly $3036$ rectangles.\\n\\n**Placement of uncovered squares.**\\n\\nPair the rows as\\n\\\\[\\n(1,2),\\\\;(3,4),\\\\;(5,6),\\\\dots,(2023,2024).\\n\\\\]\\nThere are $1012$ pairs; row $2025$ remains unpaired.\\n\\nFor the $k$‑th pair $(2k-1,2k)$ ($k=1,2,\\\\dots,1012$) place the two uncovered squares in the odd columns\\n\\\\[\\n(2k-1,\\\\,2k-1)\\\\quad\\\\text{and}\\\\quad(2k,\\\\,2k+1).\\n\\\\]\\nThus the two holes of a pair are separated by exactly one even column, namely column $2k$.  All odd columns $1,3,5,\\\\dots,2025$ are used for holes; the even columns $2,4,\\\\dots,2024$ are *gaps*.\\n\\nFor the single unpaired row $2025$ put its uncovered square in column $2$ (any even column would work; we choose $2$ for convenience).\\n\\nThe resulting permutation $\\\\pi$ is a bijection, so the condition “exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column’’ is satisfied.\\n\\n**Tiling.**\\n\\nConsider a paired block $B_k$ consisting of rows $2k-1$ and $2k$.\\n\\n- *Middle rectangle.*  Column $2k$ (the even column between the two holes) is free of holes for the rows of $B_k$.  Hence the $2\\\\times1$ rectangle\\n  \\\\[\\\\mathcal M_k = \\\\{(2k-1,2k),(2k,2k)\\\\}\\\\]\\n  can be placed.\\n\\n- *Left rectangle.*  All columns strictly left of the leftmost hole of the block are $1,2,\\\\dots,2k-2$.  None of these columns contain a hole belonging to rows $2k-1$ or $2k$, so the rectangle\\n  \\\\[\\\\mathcal L_k = \\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid i\\\\in\\\\{2k-1,2k\\\\},\\\\;1\\\\le j\\\\le 2k-2\\\\}\\\\]\\n  is admissible.  For $k=1$ the left side is empty and $\\\\mathcal L_1$ is omitted.\\n\\n- *Right rectangle.*  All columns strictly right of the rightmost hole of the block are $2k+2,2k+3,\\\\dots,2025$.  Again none of these columns contain a hole for rows $2k-1,2k$, so we may place\\n  \\\\[\\\\mathcal R_k = \\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid i\\\\in\\\\{2k-1,2k\\\\},\\\\;2k+2\\\\le j\\\\le2025\\\\}.\\n  \\\\]\\n  For $k=1012$ the right side is empty and $\\\\mathcal R_{1012}$ is omitted.\\n\\nThus each interior pair ($2\\\\le k\\\\le1011$) uses three rectangles, while the first and last pair use only two 