f7.21.1]) states:\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  Let $F$ be a Ferrers diagram with row\u2011lengths $\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\dots\\ge\\lambda_k>0$.  The minimum number of rectangles needed to partition $F$ equals the number of *distinct* row\u2011lengths $\\lambda_i$.\n\nThe proof is a simple greedy algorithm: start with the topmost row, take a maximal rectangle of its full length, delete it, and repeat.  No rectangle can be merged across a change of row\u2011length, so the bound is tight.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Lower bound for any permutation\n\nLet $p$ be any permutation of $[n]$ and let $d(p)$ denote the number of **distinct** values among the set $\\{p(1),p(2),\\dots,p(n)\\}$.  Of course $d(p)=n$, but we shall apply Lemma\u202f1 to the *ordered* sequences of hole positions.\n\n* Sort the rows so that $p(i_1)\\ge p(i_2)\\ge\\dots\\ge p(i_n)$.  The row\u2011lengths of $L(p)$ are precisely $p(i_1)-1,\\;p(i_2)-1,\\dots, p(i_n)-1$.  The number of distinct row\u2011lengths of $L(p)$ is therefore the number of distinct values of $p(i)$, i.e. $n$.\n* Similarly, after sorting rows in increasing order of $p(i)$, the row\u2011lengths of $R(p)$ are $n-p(i)-1$, whose distinct values are also $n$.\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f1 to $L(p)$ and $R(p)$ separately gives the following immediate bound.\n\n> **Corollary 2.**  For every permutation $p$,\n> \\[\n>   \\text{any tiling of }[n]^2\\setminus\\{(i,p(i))\\}\\text{ uses at least }2n-2\\text{ rectangles.}\n> \\]\n> *Proof.*  $L(p)$ needs at least $n-1$ rectangles because the smallest row\u2011length is $0$ (the row whose hole lies in column\u202f1) and the largest is $n-1$ (the row whose hole lies in column\u202f$n$); the $0$\u2011length rows do not contribute a rectangle, so we lose exactly one.  The same argument for $R(p)$ yields another $n-1$ rectangles.  The two families are disjoint, hence at least $2n-2$ rectangles in total.  \u220e\n\nThus for $n=2025$ we have the universal lower bound\n\\[\n  \\tau(2025)\\;\\ge\\;2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n## 4. Constructing a tiling that meets the bound\n\nWe now exhibit a permutation $p$ and an explicit tiling with exactly $2n-2$ rectangles.  This shows the lower bound is tight, i.e. $\\tau(n)=2n-2$ for all $n\\ge2$.\n\n### 4.1 Choice of permutation\n\nTake the **reverse permutation**\n\\[\n  p(i)=n+1-i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\nThus the uncovered cells lie on the anti\u2011diagonal.\nThe left region $L(p)$ consists of the cells strictly *below* the anti\u2011diagonal, while $R(p)$ consists of the cells strictly *above* it.\nBoth regions are Ferrers diagrams with a very regular shape:\n* After ordering rows from top to bottom (i.e. in the natural order), the row\u2011lengths of $L(p)$ are $0,1,2,\\dots,n-1$.\n* After ordering rows from bottom to top, the row\u2011lengths of $R(p)$ are also $0,1,2,\\dots,n-1$.\nHence each of $L(p)$ and $R(p)$ has exactly $n-1$ distinct non\u2011zero row\u2011lengths.\n\n### 4.2 Tiling the lower region $L(p)$\n\nConsider $L(p)$.  For each $k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots,n-1\\}$ define the rectangle\n\\[\n  A_k=\\{\\,i\\mid i\\ge k+1\\}\\times\\{\\,j\\mid 1\\le j\\le k\\\\}.\n\\]\nGeometrically $A_k$ is the maximal rectangle whose right side ends just before the anti\u2011diagonal in rows $k+1,\\dots,n$.  The family $\\{A_k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1\\}$ consists of $n-1$ pairwise disjoint rectangles whose union is exactly $L(p)$.  No larger rectangle can be added because any rectangle that would contain two consecutive values of $k$ would necessarily cross the anti\u2011diagonal and thus contain a hole.\n\n### 4.3 Tiling the upper region $R(p)$\n\nSymmetrically, for each $k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots,n-1\\}$ define\n\\[\n  B_k=\\{\\,i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-k\\}\\times\\{\\,j\\mid n-k+1\\le j\\le n\\\\}.\n\\]\nThese $n-1$ rectangles are disjoint, lie entirely above the anti\u2011diagonal, and together cover $R(p)$.\n\n### 4.4 Counting the rectangles\n\nThe tiling $\\mathcal R=\\{A_1,\\dots,A_{n-1},B_1,\\dots,B_{n-1}\\}$ uses exactly $2n-2$ rectangles.