    "solution": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have found a complete solution. The minimum number of rectangular tiles required is exactly $4048$.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**\n1. **Permutation model.** The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix: one uncovered cell in each row and each column.\n2. **Ferrers decomposition.** For a permutation $p$, split the covered cells into the left region $L(p)=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\}$ and the right region $R(p)=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}$. After sorting rows, each region becomes a Ferrers (Young) diagram.\n3. **Lower bound via a Ferrers\u2011diagram lemma.** A Ferrers diagram whose rows have $k$ distinct non\u2011zero lengths needs at least $k$ rectangles to partition it. Applying this to $L(p)$ and $R(p)$ (each has $n-1$ distinct non\u2011zero row lengths) yields a universal lower bound of $2n-2$ rectangles.\n4. **Achieving the bound.** For any permutation $p$, tile each row $i$ with up to two horizontal strips: the cells left of the hole $(i,p(i))$ (if any) and the cells right of the hole (if any). These strips are disjoint rectangles, covering every non\u2011hole cell. The total number of strips is $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$.\n5. **Conclusion.** The lower bound equals the construction, so the minimum is $2n-2$. For $n=2025$ this equals $4048$.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n#### 1. Formalisation of the problem\nLet $n=2025$.  A placement of tiles satisfying the statement can be described combinatorially as follows.\n\n* Choose a permutation $p\\colon [n]\\to[n]$ where $[n]=\\{1,2,\\dots,n\\}$.  The cell $(i,p(i))$ is the unique uncovered cell in row $i$ and column $p(i)$.  Thus the set of uncovered cells is $U=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\}$.\n* Every other cell of the $n\\times n$ board must be covered by a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines, and no rectangle may contain a cell of $U$.\n* A **tiling** is a family $\\mathcal R$ of such rectangles that are pairwise interior\u2011disjoint and whose union is exactly $[n]^2\\setminus U$.\n\nThe problem asks for\n\\[\n  \\tau(n)=\\min_{p\\in S_n}\\bigl\\{\\,|\\mathcal R|\\mid \\mathcal R\\text{ is a tiling for }p\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\nWe shall determine $\\tau(2025)$.\n\n#### 2. Splitting the board into two Ferrers diagrams\nFor a fixed permutation $p$ define\n\\[\n  L(p)=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j<p(i)\\},\\qquad\n  R(p)=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\nClearly $[n]^2\\setminus U = L(p)\\cup R(p)$ and $L(p)\\cap R(p)=\\varnothing$.\n\nIf we reorder the rows of $L(p)$ in **decreasing** order of $p(i)$, the row\u2011lengths become\n\\[\n  \\ell_i = p(i)-1,\\qquad i=1,\\dots,n.\n\\]\nSince $p$ is a permutation, the multiset $\\{\\ell_i\\}$ is exactly $\\{0,1,2,\\dots,n-1\\}$.  Consequently, after this ordering $L(p)$ is a left\u2011justified Ferrers diagram (also called a Young diagram) whose rows have lengths $n-1,n-2,\\dots,0$.\n\nSimilarly, if we reorder the rows of $R(p)$ in **increasing** order of $p(i)$, the row\u2011lengths become\n\\[\n  r_i = n-p(i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots,n,\n\\]\nwhich again form the set $\\{0,1,\\dots,n-1\\}$.  Thus $R(p)$ is a right\u2011justified Ferrers diagram with the same list of row lengths.\n\nHence both $L(p)$ and $R(p)$ are Ferrers diagrams whose non\u2011zero row lengths are all distinct and range from $1$ to $n-1$.\n\n#### 3. A lemma on rectangle partitions of Ferrers diagrams\n\n**Lemma 1.** *Let $F$ be a Ferrers diagram.  Let $d$ be the number of distinct non\u2011zero row lengths of $F$.  Any partition of $F$ into axis\u2011parallel rectangles uses at least $d$ rectangles, and $d$ rectangles are sufficient.*\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a rectangle $R$ lies inside $F$ and covers two rows whose lengths are $\\ell$ and $\\ell'$, with $\\ell<\\ell'$.  Because $F$ is left\u2011justified, the shorter row ends at column $\\ell$, while the longer row continues beyond column $\\ell$.  The rectangle $R$, being a Cartesian product of an interval of rows and an interval of columns, would then contain a cell in the longer row at a column $c>\\ell$ that does **not** belong to the shorter row; that cell lies outside $F$, contradicting $R\\subseteq F$.  Therefore each rectangle can cover only rows of equal length.  Consequently at least one rectangle is needed for each distinct non\u2011zero row length, giving the lower bound $d$.\n\nConversely, for each distinct non\u2011zero length $\\ell$ collect all rows of $F$ having that length; they form a contiguous block of rows (by the definition of a Ferrers diagram).  The Cartesian product of this block of rows with the first $\\ell$ columns is a rectangle contained in $F$, and the collection of these rectangles (one per distinct length) partitions $F$.  Hence $d$ rectangles suffice.  \u220e\n\n#### 4. Lower bound for any permutation\nApplying Lemma\u00a01 to $L(p)$ we have $d_{L}=n-1$ (the non\u2011zero lengths are $1,2,\\dots,n-1$).  Thus any rectangle partition of $L(p)$ needs at least $n-1$ rectangles.  The same argument applied to $R(p)$ yields another $n-1$ rectangles.  Since $L(p)$ and $R(p)$ are disjoint, a tiling of $[n]^2\\setminus U$ requires at least\n\\[\n  (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\nrectangles.  Therefore\n\\[\n  \\tau(n)\\ge 2n-2.\\tag{1}\n\\]\nFor $n=2025$ this gives $\\tau(2025)\\ge 4048$.\n\n#### 5. An explicit tiling attaining the bound\nFix an arbitrary permutation $p$.  For each row $i$ we construct up to two horizontal rectangles:\n\n* **Left strip** (if $p(i)>1$):\n  \\[\\mathcal L_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le p(i)-1\\}.\\]\n  This is a $1\\times(p(i)-1)$ rectangle covering all cells of row $i$ that lie left of the uncovered cell $(i,p(i))$.\n* **Right strip** (if $p(i)<n$):\n  \\[\\mathcal R_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\\]\n  This is a $1\\times(n-p(i))$ rectangle covering all cells of row $i$ that lie right of the uncovered cell.\n\nThe collection\n\\[\n  \\mathcal T = \\bigl\\{\\mathcal L_i\\mid p(i)>1\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\mathcal R_i\\mid p(i)<n\\bigr\\}\n\\]\nconsists of axis\u2011parallel rectangles whose interiors are pairwise disjoint:\n* Different rows are disjoint because they occupy distinct $i$\u2011coordinates.\n* Within a single row the left and right strips are separated by the uncovered cell, so they do not overlap.\n* By construction no strip contains the uncovered cell of its row.\nThus $\\mathcal T$ is a valid tiling of $[n]^2\\setminus U$.\n\nHow many rectangles does $\\mathcal T$ contain?  Exactly one left strip for every row except the unique row where $p(i)=1$, and exactly one right strip for every row except the unique row where $p(i)=n$.  Hence\n\\[\n  |\\mathcal T| = (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\tag{2}\n\\]\nCombining (1) and (2) we obtain\n\\[\n  \\tau(n)=2n-2.\n\\]\nFor $n=2025$ this yields\n\\[\n  \\tau(2025)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\nThus $4048$ is both a lower bound and an attainable number of tiles, and therefore it is the minimum possible.\n\n#### 6. Conclusion\nThe minimum number of rectangular tiles required for a $2025\\times2025$ board under the given conditions is $4048$.\n",
