e two rectangles:\\n\\n*Left‑region rectangle*\\n\\\\[\\nT_k^{\\\\text{L}}=\\\\{\\\\,(r,c)\\\\mid k\\\\le r\\\\le n,\\\\; 1\\\\le c\\\\le k\\\\\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nEvery cell of $T_k^{\\\\text{L}}$ satisfies $c\\\\le k< r+1$, so $c<\\\\sigma(r)$; hence $T_k^{\\\\text{L}}\\\\subseteq L$ and it contains no uncovered square.\\n\\n*Right‑region rectangle*\\n\\\\[\\nT_k^{\\\\text{R}}=\\\\{\\\\,(r,c)\\\\mid 1\\\\le r\\\\le k,\\\\; k+1\\\\le c\\\\le n\\\\\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nHere $c\\\\ge k+1> r$, so $c>\\\\sigma(r)$; thus $T_k^{\\\\text{R}}\\\\subseteq R$ and again no uncovered square lies inside.\\n\\nThe $2(n-1)$ rectangles $\\\\{T_k^{\\\\text{L}},T_k^{\\\\text{R}}\\\\mid k=1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ are pairwise disjoint: any two left‑region rectangles have disjoint column sets, any two right‑region rectangles have disjoint row sets, and a left‑region rectangle uses only columns $\\\\le k$ while a right‑region rectangle uses only columns $\\\\ge k+1$, so a left‑ and a right‑region rectangle never intersect.\\n\\nFinally we verify that every non‑uncovered cell belongs to exactly one of these rectangles.  Let $(r,c)$ be a cell not in $U$.\\n- If $c<\\\\sigma(r)$ then $c\\\\le r$ (because $\\\\sigma(r)=r+1$ for $r<n$ and $\\\\sigma(n)=1$).  Choose $k=c$; then $k\\\\le r$ and $c\\\\le k$, so $(r,c)\\\\in T_k^{\\\\text{L}}$.\\n- If $c>\\\\sigma(r)$ then $c\\\\ge r+2$ (or $c\\\\ge2$ when $r=n$).  Choose $k=r$; then $r\\\\le k$ and $c\\\\ge k+1$, so $(r,c)\\\\in T_k^{\\\\text{R}}$.\\nThus the $2(n-1)$ rectangles cover precisely the complement of $U$.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**3.  Conclusion.**\\nThe lower bound shows that any admissible tiling needs at least $2(n-1)$ tiles, and the explicit construction above achieves exactly $2(n-1)$ tiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n2(n-1)=2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n**Answer:** 4048.\",\n  \"method\": \"analysis\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4047\n}"
