                "content": "{\n  \"solution\": \"### Summary ###\\n\\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is 2025.\\n\\n**b. Method Sketch:**\\n1. *Permutation of uncovered cells.*  Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation $\\\\sigma$ of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ (with $n=2025$) such that the uncovered squares are precisely $\\\\{(i,\\\\sigma(i))\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.\\n2. *Lower bound via rank.*  Let $J$ be the $n\\\\times n$ all‑ones matrix and let $P_{\\\\sigma}$ be the permutation matrix of $\\\\sigma$.  The matrix of the covered squares is\\n   $$A = J - P_{\\\\sigma}.$$ \\n   Since $J$ and $P_{\\\\sigma}$ commute and $P_{\\\\sigma}$ fixes the vector $(1,\\\\dots,1)^T$, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $n-1$ (once) and $-\\\\lambda$ for the $n-1$ eigenvalues $\\\\lambda$ of $P_{\\\\sigma}$ on the orthogonal complement of $(1,\\\\dots,1)^T$.  All $\\\\lambda$ are $\\\\pm1$ or complex roots of unity, hence none equals $0$.  Consequently $A$ is invertible and $\\\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$.\\n   Each rectangular tile covering a set $R\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ of rows and $C\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ of columns corresponds to the rank‑one matrix $u_R v_C^{\\\\!T}$ (the outer product of the $0\\\\!$–$1$ indicator vectors of $R$ and $C$).  If $k$ tiles cover all squares of $A$, then\\n   $$A = \\\\sum_{t=1}^{k} u_{R_t} v_{C_t}^{\\\\!T}.$$\\n   The rank of a sum does not exceed the sum of the ranks, so $\\\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\\\le k$.  Because $\\\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$, we obtain the lower bound $k\\\\ge n$.\\n3. *Construction with $n$ tiles.*  Choose any permutation $\\\\sigma$; for concreteness take the cyclic shift $\\\\sigma(i)=i+1$ for $i<n$ and $\\\\sigma(n)=1$.  For each row $i$ place a $1\\\\times (n-1)$ tile covering all squares $(i,j)$ with $j\\\\neq\\\\sigma(i)$.  The tiles lie in distinct rows, so they never overlap.  By construction row $i$ has exactly one uncovered square $(i,\\\\sigma(i))$, and column $j$ has exactly one uncovered square $(\\\\sigma^{-1}(j),j)$.  Hence the required condition is satisfied with exactly $n$ tiles.\\n4. *Conclusion.*  The lower bound $k\\\\ge n$ and the explicit construction with $k=n$ together show that the minimum possible number of tiles is $n=2025$.\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\n**1. Formalisation of the problem.**\\nLet $n=2025$.  The $n\\\\times n$ board consists of unit squares indexed by pairs $(i,j)$ with $1\\\\le i,j\\\\le n$.  A *tile* is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; equivalently a tile is a set $R\\\\times C$ where $R\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ and $C\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ are non‑empty intervals of rows and columns.  Tiles are required to be pairwise disjoint.\\nThe condition *\\\"each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square\\\"* means that there exists a set $U\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}^2$ such that\\n\\\\[\\n|U\\\\cap\\\\{i\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}|=1\\\\quad\\\\text{for every }i,\\\\qquad\\n|U\\\\cap\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{j\\\\}|=1\\\\quad\\\\text{for every }j.\\n\\\\]\\nHence $U$ consists of $n$ squares, one in each row and column.  Consequently there is a permutation $\\\\sigma\\\\in S_n$ with\\n\\\\[\\nU=\\\\{(i,\\\\sigma(i))\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe set of covered squares is the complement $C=\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}^2\\\\setminus U$.\\nOur aim is to partition $C$ into the smallest possible number of rectangles.\\n\\n**2. A linear‑algebraic lower bound.**\\nDefine the $n\\\\times n$ matrices\\n\\\\[\\nJ = (1)_{i,j}\\\\quad\\\\text{(all entries equal to $1$)},\\\\qquad\\nP_{\\\\sigma}= (p_{ij})\\\\text{ with }p_{ij}=\\\\begin{cases}1,&j=\\\\sigma(i),\\\\\\\\0,&\\\\text{otherwise.}\\\\end{cases}\\n\\\\]\\nThe matrix of the covered squares is\\n\\\\[\\nA = J - P_{\\\\sigma}.\\n\\\\]\\nBoth $J$ and $P_{\\\\sigma}$ commute because $JP_{\\\\sigma}=J=P_{\\\\sigma}J$ (multiplying by $J$ merely adds the entries of a column or a row, and each column/row of $P_{\\\\sigma}$ contains exactly one $1$).  The vector $\\\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\\\dots,1)^T$ is an eigenvector of $P_{\\\\sigma}$ with eigenvalue $1$, and $J\\\\mathbf{1}=n\\\\mathbf{1}$.  In a basis consisting of $\\\\mathbf{1}$ and $n-1$ vectors orthogonal to $\\\\mathbf{1}$, the matrix $J$ is diagonal with entries $n,0,\\\\dots,0$, while $P_{\\\\sigma}$ is diagonal with entries $1,\\\\lambda_2,\\\\dots,\\\\lambda_n$, where each $\\\\lambda_k$ is a root of unity (the eigenvalues of a permutation matrix).  Therefore\\n\\\\[\\nA\\\\text{ has eigenvalues }n-1\\\\text{ (once) and }-\\\\lambda_k\\\\text{ for }k=2,\\\\dots,n.\\n\\\\]\\nSince none of the $\\\\lambda_k$ equals $0$, all eigenvalues of $A$ are non‑zero; thus $A$ is invertible and $\\\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$.\\n\\nNow consider a rectangular tile $T=R\\\\times C$ with $R\\\\neq\\\\emptyset$, $C\\\\neq\\\\emptyset$.  Its incidence matrix $M_T$ is the outer product $u_R v_C^{\\\\!T}$ where $u_R$ (resp. $v_C$) is the $0\\\\!$–$1$ column vector indicating the rows in $R$ (resp. the columns in $C$).  Hence $\\\\operatorname{rank}(M_T)=1$.  If $k$ tiles $T_1,\\\\dots,T_k$ cover exactly the set $C$, then\\n\\\\[\\nA = \\\\sum_{t=1}^{k} M_{T_t}.\\n\\\\]\\nRank is sub‑additive, so $\\\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\\\le\\\\sum_{t=1}^{k}\\\\operatorname{rank}(M_{T_t}) = k$.  Because $\\\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$, we obtain the inequality\\n\\\\[\\nk\\\\ge n.\\n\\\\]\\nThus **any** tiling satisfying the row/column condition uses at least $n$ tiles.\\n\\n**3. An explicit tiling with $n$ tiles.**\\nChoose a permutation $\\\\sigma$ that is not the identity; for concreteness let\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sigma(i)=\\\\begin{cases}i+1,&1\\\\le i<n,\\\\\\\\1,&i=n.\\\\end{cases}\\n\\\\]\\nThe uncovered squares are $U=\\\\{(i,\\\\sigma(i))\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.  For each row $i$ place the tile\\n\\\\[\\nT_i = \\\\{i\\\\}\\\\times \\\\bigl\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}\\\\setminus\\\\{\\\\sigma(i)\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\n$T_i$ is a $1\\\\times (n-1)$ rectangle; different $T_i$ lie in different rows, so they are pairwise disjoint.  By construction row $i$ contains exactly the uncovered square $(i,\\\\sigma(i))$, and column $j$ contains exactly the uncovered square $(\\\\sigma^{-1}(j),j)$.  Consequently the tiling meets all requirements and uses exactly $n$ tiles.\\n\\n**4. Minimality.**\\nThe lower bound $k\\\\ge n$ from Section 2 and the construction of $n$ tiles from Section 3 together imply that the minimum possible number of tiles equals $n$.\\n\\n**5. Numerical answer.**\\nFor the given board $n=2025$, the minimum number of tiles is therefore\\n\\\\[\\n2025.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n---\\nThus the problem is solved.\\n\\n### End of Detailed Solution ###\",\n  \"method\": \"rank_argument\",\n  \"final_answer\": 2024\n}"
