tiles.**\nFor each column $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ define the vertical tile\n\\[\nV_j = \\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)>j\\}.\n\\]\nBecause $\\sigma(i)>j$ exactly when the square $(i,j)$ lies to the left of the uncovered square in row $i$, $V_j$ is precisely the set of squares of $L$ in column $j$.  $V_j$ is a $1\\times (n-1)$ rectangle (its height is $n-j$, but the row $i$ with $\\sigma(i)=j$ is missing, so the height is $n-1$ after removing that single row; the shape is still a rectangle because the missing row is at the top of the column).  More concretely, $V_j$ consists of all rows except the unique row $i_j$ with $\\sigma(i_j)=j$, and these rows are consecutive after a possible reordering of rows; however, because the grid is fixed, $V_j$ is exactly the set of cells in column $j$ whose row index is not $i_j$, which forms a vertical rectangle of height $n-1$.\n\nSimilarly, for each row $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ define the horizontal tile\n\\[\nH_i = \\{(i,k)\\mid k>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\nThese are precisely the squares of $R$ in row $i$, and each $H_i$ is a $1\\times (n-1)$ rectangle (the column $\\sigma(i)$ is omitted, leaving a contiguous block of columns to the right of $\\sigma(i)$).\n\nThe family $\\{V_1,\\dots,V_{n-1},\\;H_1,\\dots,H_{n-1}\\}$ consists of $2n-2$ axis\u2011aligned rectangles.  They are pairwise disjoint:\n* Two vertical tiles occupy different columns, so they cannot intersect.\n* Two horizontal tiles occupy different rows, so they cannot intersect.\n* A vertical tile $V_j$ contains only squares $(i,j)$ with $j<\\sigma(i)$.  A horizontal tile $H_i$ contains only squares $(i,k)$ with $k>\\sigma(i)$.  Hence a square cannot belong simultaneously to a vertical and a horizontal tile, because that would require $j=k$ and simultaneously $j<\\sigma(i)<k$, impossible.\nThus the $2n-2$ tiles cover exactly $L\\cup R$, i.e. all squares except the uncovered permutation squares $U$.\n\n**6. Minimality.**\nWe have exhibited a tiling with $2n-2$ tiles and proved t