]\n$L_i$ is the set of cells left of the hole in row $i$, $R_i$ the set of cells right of that hole. Because $\\pi$ is a bijection, exactly one index $i$ satisfies $\\pi(i)=1$ (hence $L_i=\\emptyset$) and exactly one index $i$ satisfies $\\pi(i)=n$ (hence $R_i=\\emptyset$). Consequently the total number of non\u2011empty sides is\n\\[\n|\\{L_i\\neq\\emptyset\\}|+|\\{R_i\\neq\\emptyset\\}| = (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n**3. Lower bound $\\boldsymbol{\\ge 2n-2}$.**\nConsider any tiling satisfying the conditions. For a tile $T$ let\n\\[\nr(T)=\\min\\{i\\mid \\exists j\\;(i,j)\\in T\\}\n\\]\nbe its topmost row. Since $T$ cannot contain the hole $(r(T),\\pi(r(T)))$, the whole rectangle $T$ must lie either entirely in $L_{r(T)}$ or entirely in $R_{r(T)}$. We assign $T$ to that side; denote the assigned side by $S(T)\\in\\{L_i,R_i\\}$.\n\n*Injectivity of the assignment.* Suppose two distinct tiles $T_1,T_2$ are assigned to the same side, say $L_i$. Then both lie in row $i$ and occupy columns $<\\pi(i)$. Because each tile is a rectangle, its projection onto row $i$ is a contiguous interval of columns. Two disjoint intervals in the same row leave at least one column of row $i$ uncovered. That uncovered cell would not be the hole (the hole is at column $\\pi(i)$), contradicting the requirement that the only uncovered cell in row $i$ is the hole. The same argument works for a common side $R_i$. Hence the map $T\\mapsto S(T)$ is injective.\n\nSince each non\u2011empty side can contain at most one tile, the number of tiles $t$ satisfies\n\\[t\\ge \\text{(number of non\u2011empty sides)} = 2n-2,\\tag{2}\\]\nby (1). Thus any feasible tiling uses at least $2n-2$ tiles.\n\n**4. Construction attaining the bound.**\nWe now exhibit a tiling with exactly $2n-2$ tiles. Choose the holes on the main diagonal:\n\\[\nH =\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nFor each $i=1,\\dots,n-1$ define a horizontal tile\n\\[\nH_i =\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a $1\\times (n-i)$ rectangle covering the cells to the right of the hole in row $i$. For each $j=1,\\dots,n-1$ define a vertical tile\n\\[\nV_j =\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a $(n-j)\\times1$ rectangle covering the cells below the hole in column $j$.\n\n*Verification.*\n- The sets $H_i$ are pairwise disjoint because they lie in distinct rows.\n- The sets $V_j$ are pairwise disjoint because they lie in distinct columns.\n- No $H_i$ intersects any $V_j$: $H_i$ consists of cells with row $i$ and column $>i$, whereas $V_j$ consists of cells with column $j$ and row $>j$. If a cell belonged to both, we would have $i=j$ and simultaneously $i>j$ and $j>i$, impossible.\n- Every cell not on the diagonal belongs either to some $H_i$ (if it lies above the diagonal) or to some $V_j$ (if it lies below the diagonal). Hence all non\u2011hole cells are covered.\n- No tile contains a diagonal cell, so the holes remain uncovered.\n\nThe number of tiles is\n\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\]\nFor $n=2025$ this equals $2\\cdot2025-2=4048$.\n\n**5. Conclusion.**\nThe lower bound (2) shows that any admissible tiling needs at least $2n-2$ tiles. The explicit construction above achieves exactly $2n-2$ tiles. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles for a $2025\\times2025$ grid is\n\\[\\boxed{4048}.\\]\n\n### End of Detailed Solution ###",
